course: myths
lesson: 5
title: Moving, swimming and sport
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The beach, a hot day, a packed lunch, and Grandma checking her watch: "Not one toe in the sea for a whole HOUR after eating, or you'll get cramp and sink like a stone!"
explain: Sport is full of rules like that, passed from coach to coach and gran to gran: crack your knuckles and you'll get arthritis (sore, stiff joints), stretch for ages or you'll snap something, no pain, no gain.
explain: Some came from old leaflets, some from adverts, and some from things that happen at the same time and look like cause and effect: a man stops training, loses muscle and gains fat, so people say his muscle "turned into" fat.
explain: Scientists have tested many of them, and one doctor even cracked the knuckles of only one hand for sixty years to find out; the knuckles on both hands came out the same.
explain: What really keeps you safe is plainer: warm up by moving, stop when pain is sharp, drink when thirsty, swim where it is supervised, and if cramp grabs you in the water, float on your back and shout.
fact: There is no evidence that eating before swimming makes people drown; the real dangers are deep or cold water and swimming with nobody watching.
fact: The pop when you crack your knuckles comes from gas bubbles in the fluid of the joint; a doctor who cracked one hand's knuckles for over sixty years found no arthritis difference between his hands.
fact: Long, still stretches before exercise don't prevent most injuries; a gentle warm-up of moving and jogging is better.
fact: Muscle and fat are different kinds of tissue, so one cannot turn into the other; muscle can shrink while fat grows at the same time.
fact: You cannot burn fat from one spot by exercising that spot; sit-ups make tummy muscles stronger but do not melt tummy fat.
fact: Tiredness and effort during sport are normal, but sharp pain is a signal to stop, because pushing on can cause injury.
fact: How much water you need depends on your size, the weather and how active you are; food and other drinks count too, so "exactly eight glasses" is a rough guess.
fact: If you get cramp in the water, you can float on your back, stretch the muscle and call for help; staying calm is the key.
fact: For ordinary children's games, water is enough; sports drinks add sugar and help mainly in long, hard exercise; adverts with famous players are made to sell drinks, not to test them.
fact: Waking a sleepwalker will not kill them, though they may be confused; it is best to guide them gently back to bed.
fact: Advice in sport changes as scientists test old ideas, so a rule that was normal thirty years ago may be dropped today.
exercise: Grandma's rule: "No swimming for one hour after eating!" What does the evidence say? | There's no evidence eating first makes people drown | Eating first makes a dangerous cramp almost certain in the pool | You must wait exactly sixty minutes | Swimming after a sandwich is safe only for adults
exercise: What are the real dangers to watch out for when swimming? | Deep or cold water and nobody watching | Eating a small snack an hour before getting in | Swimming in a bright red swimsuit in the sea | Swimming straight after waking up in the morning
exercise: Nan winces: "Stop cracking your knuckles, you'll get arthritis!" What makes the popping sound? | Gas bubbles in the fluid inside the joint | Bones rubbing together and slowly wearing away | Tiny bones in the finger snapping and healing again | Muscles tearing a little bit more each time you do it
exercise: How did one doctor test the knuckle-cracking story? | He cracked one hand's knuckles for sixty years and compared hands | He asked his patients if their knuckles hurt | He looked at knuckles under a microscope once | He cracked a friend's knuckles until they broke
exercise: The PE teacher says: "Long stretches before running or you'll get injured!" What do studies suggest is better? | A warm-up of gentle moving and jogging | Holding each stretch very still for ten minutes | No warm-up at all, ever, to save your strength | Sitting very still on the bench to save energy
exercise: Sam says muscle turns into fat if you stop exercising. What's really happening? | Muscle can shrink while fat grows, but one can't turn into the other | Muscle melts and becomes fat in about a week | Fat turns into muscle when you exercise | Muscle changes into fat only after age forty
exercise: Ruby does 100 sit-ups a day for a flat tummy. What will they really do? | Make her tummy muscles stronger, not melt fat in that spot | Burn fat only from her tummy, as long as she does enough of them | Turn tummy fat into muscle | Nothing at all for any part of her
exercise: Coach shouts: "No pain, no gain! If it hurts, keep going!" When should you stop? | When the pain is sharp | Only when you faint | Never, pain means it's working | Whenever you feel slightly tired
exercise: Jamal's app says exactly eight glasses of water a day. What is closer to the truth? | It depends on size, weather and activity, and food counts too | Everyone needs exactly eight glasses a day, no more and no less | Children need sixteen glasses a day | Water from food is bad for you
exercise: Megan's brother says cramp in the sea means you'll drown. What should you do if it happens? | Float on your back, stretch the muscle and shout for help | Swim as fast as you possibly can to the shore before it gets worse | Dive under to rub the leg | Stay still and silent so you don't sink
exercise: Ethan sees an advert: sports drinks make you run faster. What's true for a normal kids' match? | Water is enough; sports drinks mainly add sugar | Sports drinks make children twice as fast | Water slows you down during a match | You must drink a whole bottle every ten minutes
exercise: When can a sports drink actually help? | In long, hard exercise | In a ten-minute game of tag | Sitting watching a match | Before going to sleep
exercise: Priya: "Never wake a sleepwalker, it can kill them!" What is true? | It won't kill them; guide them gently back to bed | It really can stop their heart if they are woken suddenly | You must shout very loudly to wake them up as fast as you can | Splash cold water on their face right away to wake them up
exercise: Why did the "stretch for ages first" rule change? | Scientists tested it and found moving warm-ups work better | Teachers got bored of waiting | Stretching was banned in schools after too many children got hurt | Old people can't stretch any more
exercise: What is the difference between muscle and fat? | They are different kinds of tissue in the body | Fat is just lazy muscle that has stopped doing any work | Muscle is fat that has been trained | They are the same thing in different colours
test: "bro my nan made me wait a whole hour after chips before going in the pool, is that legit?" | No, there's no evidence for the hour rule, but a lifeguard and shallow water matter | Yes, swimming after chips is deadly | Yes, but only half an hour for chips | No, you can swim straight after anything, even alone in deep water
test: What would happen if you cracked the knuckles of one hand every day for years and never the other? | Very likely no difference in arthritis between the hands | The cracking hand would get arthritis first, just as Gran warned him | The other hand would get weaker from not cracking | Both hands would stop popping forever
test: Gran's still worried about your knuckle cracking. Wise answer? | "A doctor tested it for 60 years! But I'll stop as it annoys you." | "You're totally wrong, Gran, read a book." | "I'll crack them louder to prove it." | "Doctors say it makes your bones stronger."
test: Your uncle stopped going to the gym. His arms got thinner and his belly bigger. "See? Muscle's turned to fat," he says. What really happened? | Two separate changes: muscle shrank and fat grew | His muscle cells slowly turned into fat cells over the winter | His fat moved from his arms to his belly | He grew new fat out of old muscle
test: A fitness video promises "burn belly fat in 7 days with this ONE move!" What's the catch? | Fat isn't lost from one spot by working that spot | It works well, but only for children and not for grown-ups | It works only if you do your sit-ups at night before bed | It's true, but you need to do a thousand of them every day
test: Coach is in a hurry and yells "keep going!" but your knee hurts sharply. What's the wise thing to say? | "I'm not giving up, but my knee hurts sharp, can I sit one out?" | Nothing, keep running till it stops hurting | "This is stupid, I quit forever." | "Pain means it's working, push harder!"
test: In a cartoon a swimmer gets cramp and sinks instantly like a rock. What's wrong with that? | Cramp hurts but you can float on your back and call for help | Nothing, because cramp always pulls a swimmer straight under the water | Cramp only happens on land | You should swim faster to beat cramp
test: On a boiling hot sports day, how much water should you drink compared with a cold day? | More, because you sweat more | Exactly eight glasses, the same | Less, the heat fills you up | None until the end of the day
test: Your friend's mum buys sports drinks for a 20-minute park kickabout "for energy". What do you think? | Water would be fine, the drinks are mostly sugar | She's right, kids need them to have enough energy to run at all | She should buy energy drinks instead | They'd make the team win for sure
test: At a sleepover, Tom is sleepwalking towards the stairs. What should you do? | Guide him gently back to bed and tell an adult | Leave him alone, because waking a sleepwalker could kill him | Scream as loudly as you can to wake him up as fast as possible | Push him back hard so that he doesn't fall over in the dark
test: The sports-drink advert shows a famous footballer. Why should that make you a bit careful? | Adverts are made to sell things, not to test them | Footballers always tell the scientific truth | Adverts are always checked by doctors before they are shown | Famous people never take money for adverts
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between muscular and adipose tissue? | They are distinct tissues and neither is converted into the other | Muscle is converted into fat upon cessation of exercise | Fat is converted into muscle by repeated sit-ups | They constitute a single tissue of variable appearance
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the consumption of food prior to swimming. | No evidence connects it with drowning; depth, cold and supervision are the principal concerns | A period of one hour must elapse to prevent drowning | Food consumed prior to swimming invariably produces cramp | Swimming is permissible only upon an empty stomach
exam: It is given that a participant in a sporting activity experiences a sharp pain in a joint. Identify the appropriate course of action. | Cessation of the activity, since sharp pain signals possible injury | Continuation of the activity with increased effort until the pain subsides | Disregard of the pain, which indicates progress | Performance of prolonged stretches upon the joint
exam: Ascertain the correct procedure in respect of a person observed to be sleepwalking. | The person is to be guided gently back to bed | The person is to be woken abruptly by loud noise | The person is to be left alone, waking being fatal | The person is to be restrained forcibly
